Some people just don't like Sokoban:
(http://groups.google.com/group/rec.games.roguelike.nethack/browse_thread/thread/1005a7a750cc0dbd/781396cec75aad15?q=%23ifdef&rnum=239#781396cec75aad15)
And I reckon they've got a point. The Sokoban floors require specially
modified game rules that not only result in another myraid of special
cases in the code, but also can't be easily explained in the context of
the game world. And, as mentioned by someone in r.g.r.d, "fixed rules"
puzzles run contrary to a common characteristic of roguelikes and
NetHack in particular: that of allowing (_not_ penalising) multiple
approaches to a challenge. What's more, all of the individual puzzles
are hard-coded, so the original _point_ of the darned thing (which, I
can only presume, is to reward the player's puzzle-solving ability) is
rendered moot! (And, as of now, no satisfactory alternative to
hard-coded levels has been presented.)

Sure, you say, the level is optional, so don't do it if you don't want
it. But what if your objection is on an aesthetic level? What do you do
if the mere presence of Sokoban in the game undermines its integrity in
your eyes? Or, additionally, what if you just don't have the resolve to
refuse Sokoban's freebies?

Most of the other "impure" features of NetHack, namely Tourists, Kops,
Sinks and the rogue floor, can be switched off with compiler options.
Here's a patch that allows roguelike purists with compilers and
compiler know-how to do the same with Sokoban.

http://www.greyfire.org/~l/sokoban.diff

Usage: To add Sokoban to the game after applying the patch, uncomment
the "#define SOKOBAN" line in config.h

Problem: Though I managed it in dungeon.def (albeit weakly), I don't
know how to perform the equivalent of an #ifdef in the various
Makefiles. Thus, levels matching "soko?-?.lev" are still compiled, but
are unused. Also, the Sokoban entry in the data file remains - but I
don't consider that much of a problem.



